<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romanticism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Romantic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romanticism was a cultural and intellectual movement of the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries that emphasised emotion, imagination, the past and national identity. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> it was closely bound up with the loss of statehood after the partitions. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> note that Romantic literature, music and political thought helped sustain Polish national consciousness through more than a century without a state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romanticism.jpg" alt="Romanticism" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caspar David Friedrich · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caspar_David_Friedrich_-_Wanderer_above_the_Sea_of_Fog.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The modern idea of the Celts was born with Lhuyd and grew with <b>Romanticism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Modernism developed out of <b>Romanticism</b>’s revolt against the effects of the Industrial Revolution and against bourgeois values; for the literary scholar Gerald Graff its ground motive was criticism of the 19th-century bourgeois social order.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>German <b>Romanticism</b> was deeply organicist and medievalist.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the sociologist Chetan Bhatt, the forms of Hindu nationalism have roots in the second half of the 19th century, in colonial experience joined with European ideas of nation, race, Aryanism, Orientalism and <b>Romanticism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…but Zarnack's text was a lover's complaint, setting the faithfulness of the fir tree against the fickleness of a young woman, and, as folklorists of German <b>Romanticism</b> sometimes did, he may have written much or all of it himself in what he took to be a folk idiom.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-tannenbaum">O Tannenbaum</a></li></ul></section>
